# MoOS: the firewall, where a person looks for it — MoOS Settings → Security & Privacy.
#
# firewall-config is a separate GTK window. It used to sit in the application menu as
# "Firewall", outside Settings; curate_app_menu.sh now hides that menu entry, and this
# file puts the same program one click away inside the settings window instead.
#
# WHY THIS DIRECTORY AND THESE KEYS (evidence, 2026-09-24, plasma-systemsettings 6.7.5):
#   * The systemsettings binary (kinfocenter is a symlink to it) carries the UTF-16
#     string `plasma/%1/externalmodules`, and upstream's app/kcmmetadatahelpers.h
#     findExternalKCMModules() fills %1 with "systemsettings" or "kinfocenter" and
#     searches QStandardPaths::GenericDataLocation — so this file lives in
#     /usr/share/plasma/systemsettings/externalmodules/. The host's
#     /usr/share/plasma/kinfocenter/externalmodules/kcm_external_plasma-systemmonitor.desktop
#     is the same mechanism for KInfoCenter.
#   * Each file is read through KService: Name, Icon and Comment (localised), X-KDE-Weight,
#     X-KDE-System-Settings-Parent-Category, and the loader marks it IsExternalApp; the
#     sidebar item launches this file's Exec through KIO (ExternalAppModule::runExternal).
#     The binary carries "IsExternalApp", "X-KDE-System-Settings-Parent-Category" and
#     "%1 is an external application and has been automatically launched".
#   * "security-privacy" is the X-KDE-System-Settings-Category of the stock
#     /usr/share/systemsettings/categories/settings-security-privacy.desktop.
#   * X-KDE-ServiceTypes / X-KDE-ParentApp are not read by this Plasma (neither string is in
#     the binary); they are kept only to mirror upstream's KInfoCenter example.
#   * Proven with the installed binary, isolated (offscreen, no session bus, throwaway HOME,
#     this file in a throwaway XDG_DATA_DIRS entry): `systemsettings --list` lists
#     "moos-firewall - Choose which network connections this computer allows", and under
#     LANG=ar the Arabic Comment. Where it sits in the sidebar is for a live review.
#   * The loader does NOT honour TryExec, so an edition without firewall-config (ARM) would
#     show a page that does nothing. curate_app_menu.sh removes this file wherever its
#     program is missing, and its gate proves every external module left resolves.
[Desktop Entry]
Type=Service
TryExec=firewall-config
Exec=firewall-config
Icon=firewall-config
Name=Firewall
Name[ar]=الجدار الناري
Comment=Choose which network connections this computer allows
Comment[ar]=اختر اتصالات الشبكة التي يسمح بها هذا الحاسوب
X-KDE-Keywords=firewall,ports,zones,services,network,security,block,allow
X-KDE-Keywords[ar]=جدار ناري,جدار الحماية,منافذ,مناطق,خدمات,شبكة,أمان,حظر,سماح
X-KDE-System-Settings-Parent-Category=security-privacy
X-KDE-Weight=60
X-KDE-ServiceTypes=SystemSettingsExternalApp
X-KDE-ParentApp=systemsettings
